<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Zhuang of Chu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 613–591 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Zhuang of Chu (reigned 613–591 BCE) was a ruler of the State of Chu, counted among the Five Hegemons of the Spring and Autumn period. He crushed the powerful Ruo'ao lineage in 605 BCE, is said to have asked a Zhou envoy about the weight of the royal cauldrons, a sign of his ambition, and defeated Jin at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bi">Battle of Bi</a> in 597 BCE, making Chu the leading power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-zhuang-of-chu.jpg" alt="King Zhuang of Chu" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 馮夢龍 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%A5%9A%E8%8E%8A%E7%8E%8B_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E6%9D%B1%E5%91%A8%E5%88%97%E5%9C%8B%E5%85%A8%E5%BF%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
